#N canvas 0 0 1000 950 10;
#X obj 20 20 inlet;
#X text 190 20 bang = rescan: re-send /serialosc/list + /serialosc/notify to 127.0.0.1:12002. outlets: 0 = device register triple "serial name port" for every /serialosc/device reply \, 1 = parsed grid key triple "x y state" from prefix/grid.key. creation args: port (the literal UDP port this instance listens on and advertises -- see design note below) and prefix (required \, e.g. /monome - Pd abstractions cannot default a missing symbol creation arg). the prefix arg is not yet used for grid-key matching -- see not-yet-handled note.;
#X obj 400 20 loadbang;
#X obj 20 60 route bang;
#X obj 400 60 t b b;
#X msg 20 100 connect 127.0.0.1 12002;
#X obj 400 100 netreceive -u -b \$1;
#X obj 20 140 float \$1;
#X msg 20 180 list localhost \$1;
#X obj 20 220 oscformat /serialosc/list;
#X obj 150 220 oscformat /serialosc/notify;
#X obj 400 260 netsend -u -b;
#X obj 400 140 oscparse;
#X obj 400 180 route list;
#X obj 400 220 route serialosc monome;
#X obj 400 260 route device add remove;
#X obj 620 260 route grid;
#X obj 620 300 route key;
#X obj 20 500 outlet;
#X obj 150 500 outlet;
#X obj 400 340 unpack s s f;
#X obj 530 380 t b f;
#X msg 640 380 connect localhost \$1;
#X obj 640 420 netsend -u -b;
#X obj 530 420 t b;
#X obj 530 460 float \$1;
#X msg 530 500 list \$1;
#X obj 530 540 oscformat /sys/port;
#X obj 400 460 symbol \$2;
#X msg 400 500 list \$1;
#X obj 400 540 oscformat /sys/prefix;
#X text 190 340 handshake (2026-09-17 turn 3): every /serialosc/device reply is handshaken - the device's own reply port is connected on the second netsend and /sys/port port + /sys/prefix prefix are sent to it. multiple devices: each reply is handshaken and the last connect wins \, serialosc.js's first-device menu selection is not yet ported. /serialosc/add and /serialosc/remove re-trigger a rescan. confirmed against the real daemon (turn 5): they do carry the same id/type/port triple as /serialosc/device \, but that payload is discarded (converted to a bare bang via [t b]) rather than parsed -- a real add/remove could in principle skip the full rescan and update state directly from this payload \, not done yet.;
#X text 190 580 not yet handled: disconnect detection (serialosc.js treats a /sys/port reply that differs from in_port as a disconnect) \, /sys/host + /sys/info \, manual device selection \, and the /monome prefix is hardcoded into the route chain rather than using the abstraction's own prefix creation arg (\$2) -- a device configured with a non-default prefix will not have its grid-key events matched.;
#X text 190 620 design notes (2026-09-17 turn 3 \, all three confirmed empirically against a live Pd 0.56.5 install \, not by inspection alone): (1) dropped serialosc.js's "port + 12288" offset scheme -- netreceive's listening port is a creation-time argument only in Pd and cannot be rebound by sending it a computed value at runtime (a bare float into netreceive's inlet is read as an implicit 'send' call and errors "'send' only works for TCP" in UDP mode). The creation arg is now the literal port to bind and advertise directly. (2) [t b b]/[t b f]'s outlets fire right to left -- the outlet that must run first (establishing a netsend "connect" target) has to be the higher-numbered (rightmost) outlet \, or the data send races ahead of the connect and is silently dropped. (3) oscparse does not hand back a full "/serialosc/device" style address as one symbol -- it splits on "/" into separate atoms and prefixes the whole message with the literal selector "list" (e.g. "/serialosc/device ..." arrives as "list serialosc device ..."). [route /serialosc/device] therefore never matches anything. Fixed with a chain of [route list] -> [route serialosc monome] -> [route device add remove] (and [route grid] -> [route key] for the grid-key path) \, matching the "nested routing" pattern shown in Pd's own osc-format-parse-help.pd.;
#X text 190 780 status 2026-09-17 (turn 3): tests/run_handshake_e2e.sh passes all 7 checks (exit 0) against tests/fake_serialosc.py -- discovery sent and received \, /sys/port + /sys/prefix observed at the fake device \, both fake grid-key events round-tripped out this abstraction's outlet 1. verified by actually running the script \, not by reading this comment -- run it yourself before trusting this note -- things change. known cosmetic rough edge: the fake daemon replies once per request (/serialosc/list and /serialosc/notify each get their own /serialosc/device reply) \, so the handshake runs twice and the second attempt logs "netsend: already connected" -- harmless \, doesn't affect either check \, not yet fixed (would need to skip re-handshaking a serial already connected). confirmed 2026-09-17 (turn 4) against real hardware: a physical monome 64 (serial m64-0865) \, the real serialosc daemon built from source \, and a live key press session all round-tripped correctly -- device_register and real grid_key x y state events both observed.;
#X obj 900 20 inlet;
#X text 900 60 inlet 1: LED control. send a 3-float list "x y level" to set one LED's brightness (0-15 \, this device is a greyscale/varibright grid -- confirmed against serialosc's own source \, src/serialosc-device/osc/mext_methods.c: led_level_set_handler -> monome_led_level_set(x \, y \, level)). only takes effect after outlet 0 has fired at least once for this device (the handshake must have already connected the device netsend \, or this silently fails the same way an unconnected netsend send always does). turn 4 (2026-09-17): only /monome/grid/led/level/set is implemented -- no /level/all \, /level/row \, /level/col \, /level/map batch messages yet \, and like the grid-key route chain the /monome prefix is hardcoded rather than built from \$2. this specific device also has a tilt/gyroscope sensor (confirmed same source: tilt/set enables it \, events arrive at <prefix>/tilt as "sensor x y z") -- not wired up at all yet \, real next-up work.;
#X obj 900 140 oscformat /monome/grid/led/level/set;
#X obj 780 260 t b;
#X text 780 300 turn 5 bugfix: /serialosc/add and /serialosc/remove carry a data payload (same triple shape as /serialosc/device -- confirmed reproducible against the real daemon \, not assumed) \, not a bare bang. feeding that payload directly into a [float] object crashed it with "float: no method for '<serial>'" the moment a real add/remove notification arrived (took ~20s into a live session to trigger \, likely a USB reconnect blip -- this device's kernel enumeration was flaky earlier too). [t b] converts any incoming message \, payload or not \, into a genuine bang before it reaches the rescan trigger.;
